{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794827016,"nonce":"DvN1cMbzDT2nOQCukX6cjVUEw2k=","message":"Eighth nAIR slice, f9cf4628: static variables and the init function. A class's statics live in one object held by a global (class_global), so Class.x is GetGlobal then a field of the statics type: field_target now routes FIELD STATIC to a static_target, and everything that already worked on instance variables (reads, writes, compound assignment) works on statics; ++ and -- on a field (instance or static) are lowered too. Module::lower_init(classes, main) builds the $init function as genhl's fun$init does, minus the runtime's class values: make each statics object and set its global, run each class's __init__ block, run the static variable initializers (their bodies are the var fields' func roots), then call main; Module::program_classes gives the order from the TAST's OrderRec. Test: a program whose static table is base*3 (so the initializers must run in order), a static counter bumped by ++, a compound += on a static and a float static, run through the init function and then called, as lowered and after O2 and O3; the agreement test matches its static functions' kinds against genhl's. Survey: 95 to 107 of 141 bodies lower (DeltaBlue 58 to 68). Gap: the class values (name and type objects for reflection) are not built, so a program that asks a class for its name or type needs them; they are module construction and come with the runtime handoff. Next: exceptions (throw), abstracts (BytesAccess), the hl.Bytes natives, then array writes, literals and push.","files":null}]}